WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.367
-Nate, welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.567
-Thank you so much
for being here, buddy.

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.267
You got a lot going on.
You're in the middle

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.300
of The Raincheck Tour.
-Yes.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.901
-And your Netflix special,
which I texted you, I loved,

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.767
"The Greatest Average American,"
was nominated

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:15.200
for a Grammy for
Best Comedy Album.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.400
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Well...

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.000
Look, I want to say to for that,
I owe a lot to this show.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.167
You have been
unbelievable to me.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.868
I don't know if anybody --
But it's been a long time since

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.033
"Late Night with Fallon."

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.067
The Roots
have been unreal to me.

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:28.634
Everybody that works here
is unreal to me.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:30.634
"Tonight Show's" been a huge
part of my career,

00:00:30.634 --> 00:00:32.000
so I do want
to thank y'all for that.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.701
I never forget it.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:36.634
-I appreciate that, buddy.
But you're the best.

00:00:36.634 --> 00:00:37.667
You put that work in.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:38.734
I don't know how
you do all this stuff.

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:41.167
But, dude, Best Comedy Album.
Congrats.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.367
That's giant.
That's massive.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:43.868
-Oh, it's enor--
Dude, it's crazy.

00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:45.300
I mean, it was like --
Yeah, I don't even know.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.400
-That's so exciting.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.701
Well, I'm just from Old Hickory.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:49.667
You know, we're not supposed
to get that stuff.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.734
Are you getting recognized more
now that you're Grammy...?

00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:54.100
-It happens -- yeah.
It happens more --

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.734
It's all, like, gradual.
Like, it's -- it's not crazy.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:00.934
I can still go everywhere.
It's not a problem.

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.200
But, like, when I get --
Like I always take pictures.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.767
Like, I --
None of this is lost on me.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.133
I truly appreciate it.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.701
But I always want to say --
Sometimes when it happens,

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:10.300
I want people to know, like,
when you leave

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.601
after we take this picture,

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:14.501
I then have to explain who I am
to like 30 people.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:16.834
[ Laughter ]
So you put me in, like,

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.901
a whole thing --
Like, and I'll still do it,

00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:20.467
but then you leave,
and they're like,

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:21.801
"Why did you --
Why did they do that?"

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.400
They just come up and ask.
Like, "Who are you?

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.634
Why did they do that?"
And they're mad about it.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:27.934
And I'm like --
I go, "I'm a comedian."

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:28.934
They're like,
"I've never heard of you."

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:30.234
I was like, "I wasn't announc--

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:33.667
I wasn't walking around saying,
'I'm a comedian.'"

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.300
I was at the airport one time,
and this guy overheard that,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.367
and he asks me, he goes,
"Is there any money in that?"

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.934
Like he might do it.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:43.067
Like, he -- just was like,
"If he could do it,

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:45.934
I mean, obviously I'll do it
if there's money in it."

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:47.567
-Oh, my God.
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:49.601
-I was just talking
to Cate Blanchett

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:51.634
about New Year's resolutions.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:56.067
Are you that type of person?
Is 2022 new Nate?

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:57.334
-It's --
I mean, I hope so.

00:01:57.334 --> 00:01:58.567
So, I have whole thing.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.267
Do you another place
I got recognized recently?

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:02.300
McDonald's,
and that's always a tough one.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.000
If you're in McDonald's alone,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.701
and then someone's like,
"Hey, are you Nate?"

00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:09.334
You're like,
"Ah, yeah, I'm sorry."

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:10.601
You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.601 --> 00:02:12.868
'Cause you're, like,
feeling kind of bad, being at --

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:15.501
-Why?
-I was 10 minutes from my house.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.934
That guy was on a road trip.

00:02:17.934 --> 00:02:19.868
Like, where it's like
that's all he has to do.

00:02:19.868 --> 00:02:20.934
And he's like,
"Where do you live?"

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:23.701
I go,
"I live 10 minutes from here.

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:26.767
I'm here just regular day.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:28.367
Like, this is just
my lunch today."

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.167
It wasn't a special thing.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:34.400
-I mean, I was sitting there,
I was holding a popcorn bucket

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.968
of soda just like --
It's so much --

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:40.734
Like, I drink --
I love it so much.

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:43.667
And you fill it up,
and it just keeps going.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.200
It's so --
Like, when it keeps going --

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.434
It's long enough that you go,
"I've got to fix my life, dude."

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.667
And then --
-It just keeps coming.

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:51.901
-And it just keeps coming.

00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:54.334
Like, you have a long time
to think about it.

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:57.501
And then, I'll take a little sip
to make sure everything's cool.

00:02:57.501 --> 00:02:59.434
And then I'm like, "Well,
I'm gonna fill it back up."

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:01.300
You know,
I don't want to get stuck out --

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.267
What if I get lost
in the road somewhere?

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:09.133
And then, we took a picture,
and I had -- I love McDonald's.

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:11.534
We should show McDonald's
more respect, to be honest.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:12.634
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:14.367
-People go in there
with sweatpants --

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:15.501
you should have a tuxedo.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:17.334
It's the most successful
restaurant on Earth.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:18.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:20.634
It's like, it's an honor
you even open the door for me.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:22.767
I come from nothing, so...
-Yeah.

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:25.033
You feel like people should give
them more respect, but that's --

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:28.133
-Yeah.
But he can't do that much of it.

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:30.100
I think, too --
You know what's hilarious, too?

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.334
So I have a podcast,
"The Nateland Podcast."

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:33.934
Hello, folks,
to the people listening.

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:37.067
But they -- it's --
I told that --

00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:40.501
My mouth has been dry lately,
and I said it on the podcast,

00:03:40.501 --> 00:03:42.968
and they were like, you probably
have type 2 diabetes.

00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:46.767
So I was in that McDonald's.
When that guy came up to me,

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:50.901
I was googling how to check
for diabetes at home.

00:03:50.901 --> 00:03:54.501
In a McDonald's,
just googling that.

00:03:54.501 --> 00:03:57.300
Just at a low point, you know?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.167
And it's -- But that was like --
But I had to check it.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:05.968
-You're googling that while
you're getting a 98-ounce soda.

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:08.534
-Yeah, and type 2
is the earned kind.

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:10.467
That's not --
You know, type 1 --

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:11.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.734 --> 00:04:16.234
You got to, you know...

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:18.634
You got to clock in
and clock out, every day.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.701
-Gotta put the work in.
-You show up, dude.

00:04:20.701 --> 00:04:22.300
They don't just give it to you.
You know?

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.567
You got to get after it.
I found out --

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:28.467
But I did an at-home test
before I came here,

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:29.701
'cause I wanted --

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:31.133
You have to, like,
stab your finger, you know?

00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:32.300
-Yeah, sure.
-And my wife did it,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.834
and she did it pretty hard.
Like, it's --

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:36.400
'Cause I couldn't do it.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.167
So I had her do it
with the little thing.

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:41.133
And I mean,
it was just the most blood.

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:43.501
I was like,
"How deep do you want to go?"

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:45.868
You know?
And I am --

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:49.634
But I was on -- I'm, like,
the beginning of pre-diabetic.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.267
Like, I'm right on the --
I'm a little normal.

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:53.968
It's like -- it'd be like
if there was, like,

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:57.133
a "No trespassing" sign,
and you walked in,

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:58.767
and you heard a gunshot,
and you're like,

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.701
you can either just go be normal
or you can be like,

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:03.000
"Well, I'm gonna go see
where that gunshot came from."

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.234
You know, like --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.234
I'm right there
on the edge of -- yeah.

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:08.501
-Yeah, with your diabetes.
-That's how I look at diabetes.

00:05:08.501 --> 00:05:12.133
-Are you cutting back on sugar?

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:13.601
-I don't know, man.
I had gummy --

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.934
[ Laughter ]
I do need to.

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:17.167
I'm reading a book about it,

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:18.667
'cause I do think
I'm addicted to sugar.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:22.434
So I eat ice cream every night.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.801
And it's so much ice cream.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:27.033
I like ketchup.
I like to use enough ketchup

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:29.067
that the person next to me
has to go,

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:31.400
"Man, that's a lot of ketchup."
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.167
Like that's --
[ Applause ]

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:37.934
They have to feel like
they have to get involved.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.334
They're like,
"That's a lot, isn't it?"

00:05:39.334 --> 00:05:41.033
And I'm like, "Is it?
I don't know."

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:42.834
-Wow.
-I'm not -- yeah.

00:05:42.834 --> 00:05:44.601
I don't --
I eat --

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:47.367
I ate gummy bears last night
in the hotel.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:51.300
And I didn't want to,
but I love them so much.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.467
[ Laughter ]
-Nate, this is terrible.

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:57.067
-I love french toast
and pancakes,

00:05:57.067 --> 00:06:00.067
and I eat it every day,
and it makes me so tired.

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:02.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:05.100
I don't know,
and I never blamed the pancakes.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.133
I'm like,
"What is up today, man?

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:10.501
Like I -- I guess
I didn't get sleep last night."

00:06:10.501 --> 00:06:12.300
[ Laughter ]
I'm going to bed,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.133
and immediately I have to go --

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:18.467
'Cause I went to the doctor,
like, to get a physical,

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:20.033
So they go through it,
they're like,

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:21.968
"What's your family history?"
Like, I don't --

00:06:21.968 --> 00:06:23.701
I've never asked, like --

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.400
How do I know
what my family history is?

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.234
Just on the holidays, be like,
"All right, raise your hand

00:06:28.234 --> 00:06:30.100
if you got diabetes
in the room. Let's go.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.267
Let's just go through everybody.

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:33.300
How many heart attacks
we got in the room?

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.701
I got a physical coming up."
-That's a bummer, yeah.

00:06:35.701 --> 00:06:38.767
But how is mom and dad?
How are your parents doing?

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:42.367
-The parents are great,
and everybody's doing great.

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:43.767
My parents are downsizing
to a --

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:46.868
they got a home with zero steps.

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:48.100
That's what
they've been looking for.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.801
-Yeah.
-They were -- They wanted --

00:06:49.801 --> 00:06:51.501
They said, "Zero steps,
or if we can find a place

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:53.400
where we could always walk down
would be great."

00:06:53.400 --> 00:07:00.133
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.133 --> 00:07:02.167
-Yeah, exactly.
-We found them one

00:07:02.167 --> 00:07:04.801
with zero steps,
and so my mom --

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:06.901
she's at the age
she wants a koi pond.

00:07:06.901 --> 00:07:08.634
There's an age
you never want one,

00:07:08.634 --> 00:07:09.834
then you hit an age,
and you're like,

00:07:09.834 --> 00:07:11.534
"That's all I've ever wanted."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.534 --> 00:07:15.000
So we were gonna go get her
a koi pond for --

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.601
We did one for this past
Mother's Day.

00:07:16.601 --> 00:07:19.167
And so we were gonna go dig
the hole and do it ourself,

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:21.801
and, dude, I don't know --
Have you ever dug a hole before?

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:24.868
Because it's maybe the hardest
thing you ever do in your life.

00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:26.200
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.667
You see it on TV and movies,
and you're like,

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:29.634
"I'll just dig this hole real
fast," and you start doing it,

00:07:29.634 --> 00:07:31.868
you're like, "I guess it's all
CGI," because it's impossible.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:33.467
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:35.601
You can't --
It takes forever.

00:07:35.601 --> 00:07:37.801
And you know it did?
It made me realize, I was like,

00:07:37.801 --> 00:07:39.501
"This is why so many people
get caught,

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:41.734
when they kill someone,
with a shallow grave."

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:44.033
That's how hard
digging a hole is.

00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:46.133
Even when you're digging
the most important hole

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:48.667
of your life...
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:50.300
...it's so hard that
you're like, "All right.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.834
I think that's good enough
right there."

00:07:51.834 --> 00:07:54.133
You know...
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:56.400
-"No one will ever find him."
-Someone's like, "Oh."

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.868
-Cover it up.
-"Could be..."

00:07:57.868 --> 00:07:59.133
They're like,
"Well, his hand's sticking out,"

00:07:59.133 --> 00:08:00.567
and you're like,
"Put a leaf over it, dude!

00:08:00.567 --> 00:08:02.667
No one's gonna come up here."
-Cover the -- yeah.

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:05.400
It's so hard that,
I swear to you,

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.467
my mom's koi pond
is aboveground.

00:08:07.467 --> 00:08:09.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:09.334 --> 00:08:11.167
-An aboveground koi pond.
-Aboveground koi pond.

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:13.868
-You're welcome, Mom.
Nate Bargatze, thank you so much

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:16.567
for coming on our show
every time.

00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:20.334
"The Greatest Average American"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:08:20.334 --> 00:08:23.667
Check out natebargatze.com
for tickets.

00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:25.467
See him live
on The Raincheck Tour!

